---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { DUAS } from "../lib/content";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const withTurkish = DUAS.filter((d) => d.translations.some((t) => t.lang === "tr")).length;
const excerpts = DUAS.filter((d) => d.source.type === "quran" && d.source.excerpt).length;
const needsReview = DUAS.filter((d) => d.reviewStatus === "needs-review").length;
---

<Base
  title="Sources and method — The Duʿāʾ of the Prophets"
  description="Where the Arabic comes from, how it is verified, who translated what, and how to report an error."
>
  <h1 style="font-size: var(--t-xl); margin-top: var(--s-12)">Sources and method</h1>

  <div class="stack prose" style="margin-top: var(--s-8)">
    <section>
      <h2 class="section-label">The Arabic</h2>
      <p>
        No Arabic on this site was typed by a person. Each entry records which
        āyāt it draws on and, where the supplication sits inside a longer verse,
        which words within them. The text is fetched from the
        <code>quran-uthmani</code> edition at build time and checked against a
        SHA-256 recorded in <code>content.lock.json</code>. If a single character
        changes, the build fails.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is deliberately stricter than fetching once and saving the result.
        An audit of the previous version of this site found two entries whose
        Arabic had drifted from the verse they cited — one carrying a different
        verse entirely, one with words inserted at an excerpt boundary. Both read
        fluently and both had citations that looked right. Only a checksum
        catches that.
      </p>
      <p>
        {excerpts} of {DUAS.length} entries are excerpts, where narrative framing
        has been trimmed so the supplication stands alone. Every one says so, in
        the margin, next to the text.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="section-label">Ḥadīth</h2>
      <p>
        There is no ḥadīth equivalent of the Qurʾān API — nothing serving graded,
        versioned, machine-checkable text. So the five Sunnah entries are the one
        place Arabic is hand-entered, and they carry a visible notice saying so.
        Each records its collection, number, grading, and who gave that grading.
        Their reference numbers await verification against a printed edition.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="section-label">Translations</h2>
      <p>
        English for the Qurʾānic entries is Marmaduke Pickthall's and Turkish is
        Elmalılı Hamdi Yazır's. Both are in the public domain, and both are
        fetched per-āyah, which means they always cover exactly the same extent
        as the Arabic beside them.
      </p>
      <p>
        That per-āyah point fixed a real problem. This project's own Turkish had
        been written one āyah at a time against an older version of the site. On
        entries that span several āyāt — 20:25–28, 14:36–41 — it covered only
        the first, so a reader saw four āyāt of Arabic and a single sentence of
        Turkish.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>There is exactly one translation per language on each page.</strong>{" "}
        Showing two renderings of the same scripture side by side asks the
        reader to adjudicate between them, which is not a question a reader
        should be handed. The page says plainly where the one it shows came
        from.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the ḥadīth entries there is no public-domain translation to fetch,
        so the Turkish is this project's own, attributed to its translator, and
        the English is a project draft labelled as unreviewed.
      </p>
      <p>
        All {DUAS.length} entries carry both English and Turkish. Every
        translation names its translator; anything unattributed does not ship.
        You can choose which languages to show in Settings.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="section-label">Material outside the Qurʾān and Sunnah</h2>
      <p>
        A few prophets the Qurʾān names have no supplication quoted in it.
        Dāwūd is the clearest case: 38:24 reports that he sought forgiveness and
        prostrated, but does not quote his words, and 2:250 is the collective
        duʿāʾ of those who crossed the river with Ṭālūt.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where a supplication is attributed to such a prophet in a scripture
        outside the Islamic tradition, it may appear here — clearly labelled, in
        its own visual treatment, never mixed into the Qurʾānic entries, and
        never presented as revelation. Islamic belief holds that the Zabūr was
        given to Dāwūd; it does not hold that the received Book of Psalms is
        that revelation preserved. Such passages carry no chain of transmission
        and no scriptural authority in Islam.
      </p>
      <p>
        Selection is deliberately narrow: a passage is included only if it is a
        supplication in form, is traditionally attributed to that prophet, and
        contains nothing contradicting Islamic belief about God. The text is
        fetched from the World English Bible, which is public domain — the same
        rule applies as everywhere else here, that scripture is never written
        from memory.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="section-label">What is not here</h2>
      <p>
        There are no claims about the reward or virtue of reciting a particular
        duʿāʾ a particular number of times. Where such claims have a sound,
        graded narration behind them they belong on a site like this; where they
        do not, they are invention, and inventing them about scripture is worse
        than omitting them. None have been written.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where this project offers its own thought on applying a duʿāʾ, it is set
        apart visually and labelled as an editorial note rather than transmitted
        knowledge.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="section-label">Review status</h2>
      <p>
        {needsReview} entries are marked as awaiting review — in most cases
        because the occasion described needs a tafsīr citation attached. The
        Arabic and its reference are verified independently of that.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="section-label">Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        A site making claims about scripture needs a way to be told it is wrong.
        If you find an error — in the Arabic, a reference, a translation, or a
        context — please
        <a href="https://github.com/CandanUmut/dua/issues/new">open an issue</a>.
        Corrections to the text itself are the highest priority and will be acted
        on before anything else.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="section-label">Offline</h2>
      <p>
        The whole site is static and small. After your first visit it works
        without a connection.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
